{
 "sheet": 1,
 "name": "MICS-5524",
 "kind": "sensor",
 "manufacturer": "SGX Sensortech",
 "title": "MiCS-5524: heated MEMS metal-oxide gas sensor for CO, natural gas and reducing gases",
 "source": {
  "document": "1084",
  "revision": "rev 8",
  "pages": 5
 },
 "summary": "A metal-oxide (MOS) gas sensor on a micromachined silicon diaphragm, in a 5 × 7 × 1.55 mm surface-mount package with ten castellated pads. A heater resistor in the diaphragm warms a sensing film whose resistance drops as reducing gases reach it; the sensor answers carbon monoxide from 1 to 1000 ppm, and also ethanol, hydrogen, ammonia and methane. The heater runs from a 5 V supply through an 82 Ω series resistor at about 76 mW, and the film is read as a resistance, through a load resistor of 820 Ω or more. Intended uses are indoor CO and natural-gas leak detection, indoor air quality, breath checking and early fire detection.",
 "key_figures": [
