 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The sensing die is a micromachined silicon diaphragm carrying a buried heater resistor (Rh1 to Rh2), with the gas-sensitive metal-oxide film (Rs1 to Rs2) laid over it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heater drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "From a 5 V supply (4.9 to 5.1 V), an 82 Ω resistor in series with the heater sets it to its working point: about 2.4 V and 32 mA, 76 mW nominal (71 to 81 mW), heater resistance 66 to 82 Ω. Heater dissipation must stay at or below 88 mW; driving the heater above its rated maximum burns the sensor out."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reading the sensor",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The film is a resistance, 100 to 1500 kΩ in clean air (R0). It is read by putting it in series with a load resistor across the 5 V supply and taking the voltage on the load resistor. The load resistor must be 820 Ω or larger, and the film's own dissipation must not exceed 8 mW. Reducing gases lower the resistance: at 60 ppm CO the ratio of resistance in air to resistance in gas, S60, is between 1.2 and 50."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gas response",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With the heater on continuously at 25 °C and 50 % RH, Rs/R0 falls with rising concentration for each gas. CO falls fastest, to 0.01 near 1000 ppm. Response is given for ethanol, hydrogen and ammonia from about 1 ppm, for H2S from 0.1 to 0.5 ppm, and for methane, propane and iso-butane from about 3000 to 15000 ppm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling and assembly",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Reflow the sensor in a neutral atmosphere with no flux vapour present. Keep it away from high levels of organic solvents, silicone vapour and cigarette smoke, which poison the sensing film. Fit it behind a filter that keeps out splashed water and dust. Handle it with ESD precautions."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packing",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Sensors ship in 16 mm carrier tape on reels: up to 700 on a 178 mm reel, or up to 2000 on a 330 mm reel."
  }
